{"step_index":162,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T17:14:14Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-06T19:14:14+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-06T19:14:14+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/Madziva/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/qualification-verification-system/docs/TECHNICAL_REPORT_TEMPLATE.md`\nTotal Lines: 166\nTotal Bytes: 10980\nShowing lines 71 to 166\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n71: |         (PostgreSQL in Docker / H2 In-Memory)               |\n72: +-------------------------------------------------------------+\n73: ```\n74: \n75: ### 3.2 Architectural Decision Records (ADRs)\n76: - **ADR-01: Adoption of Java Spring Boot 3.x**\n77:   - *Rationale:* Provides enterprise-grade dependency injection, robust security modules, seamless JPA integration, and native support for automated integration testing.\n78: - **ADR-02: Cryptographic SHA-256 Fingerprinting over Centralized Signature Authority**\n79:   - *Rationale:* Allows instant client-side or server-side tamper detection without requiring external Certificate Authority subscriptions, achieving high performance and zero dependency overhead.\n80: - **ADR-03: Multi-Stage Containerization**\n81:   - *Rationale:* Separates the Maven compilation environment from the final runtime container, reducing container image size by over 70% and minimizing the attack surface.\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 4. DevOps Implementation & CI/CD Pipeline\n86: \n87: ### 4.1 Git Branching Strategy\n88: The project follows a modified GitFlow branching model:\n89: - `main`: Production-ready branch. Direct commits are restricted; merges require approved PRs and passing CI pipeline checks.\n90: - `develop`: Integration branch where features are combined and verified.\n91: - `feature/<feature-name>`: Ephemeral branches created by team members to implement specific user stories.\n92: - `bugfix/<bug-name>`: Targeted branches for defect resolution.\n93: \n94: ### 4.2 Automated CI/CD Pipeline Workflow (GitHub Actions)\n95: The automated workflow is structured into four sequential and gated jobs:\n96: \n97: ```mermaid\n98: graph TD\n99:     A[Git Push / PR Event] --> B[Job 1: Build & Lint]\n100:     B -->|Checkstyle Passed| C[Job 2: Test & Coverage Gate]\n101:     C -->|JaCoCo >80% Passed| D[Job 3: Package Application JAR]\n102:     D -->|Artifact Generated| E[Job 4: Docker Build & Security Verification]\n103: ```\n104: \n105: 1. **Job 1: Build & Lint (`build-and-lint`):**\n106:    - Checks out code and provisions JDK 17.\n107:    - Executes `mvn checkstyle:check` to enforce coding style and convention rules.\n108: 2. **Job 2: Automated Testing & Coverage (`test-and-coverage`):**\n109:    - Executes JUnit 5 and Mockito test suites.\n110:    - Runs `mvn jacoco:check` to enforce the >80% coverage threshold. If coverage drops, the pipeline fails automatically.\n111:    - Uploads Surefire test reports and JaCoCo HTML artifacts.\n112: 3. **Job 3: Packaging (`package-application`):**\n113:    - Compiles executable Spring Boot `.jar` and stores it as a versioned GitHub Actions artifact.\n114: 4. **Job 4: Docker Containerization (`docker-build`):**\n115:    - Executes multi-stage Docker build to package the application in an unprivileged JRE 17 Alpine image.\n116: \n117: ---\n118: \n119: ## 5. Software Quality Assurance & Verification Strategy\n120: \n121: ### 5.1 Testing Pyramid Implementation\n122: - **Unit Testing (JUnit 5 & Mockito):** Tests individual service methods in isolation with mocked dependencies (e.g. `CryptoHashServiceTest`, `VerificationServiceTest`).\n123: - **Integration Testing (Spring Boot MockMvc):** Tests full HTTP request/response lifecycles, security filter intercepts, and database transactions (e.g. `VerificationControllerIntegrationTest`).\n124: - **Acceptance & Edge Case Testing:**\n125:   - Valid qualification verification -> Returns `GENUINE_AND_VALID`.\n126:   - Altered student metadata -> Cryptographic mismatch flagged as `SUSPICIOUS_OR_ALTERED`.\n127:   - Revoked qualification lookup -> Flagged as `REVOKED_CREDENTIAL` with reason.\n128:   - Unregistered serial query -> Returned as `RECORD_NOT_FOUND`.\n129: \n130: ### 5.2 Quality Gate & Coverage Evidence\n131: - **JaCoCo Line Coverage Target:** $\\ge 80\\%$\n132: - **Checkstyle Rules:** 0 critical errors allowed.\n133: \n134: ---\n135: \n136: ## 6. Deployment & Infrastructure as Code\n137: \n138: ### 6.1 Containerization Strategy\n139: A multi-stage `Dockerfile` is implemented:\n140: - **Build Stage:** `maven:3.9.6-eclipse-temurin-17` compiles dependencies and packages the binary.\n141: - **Runtime Stage:** `eclipse-temurin:17-jre-alpine` runs the application as a non-root `appuser` for security hardening.\n142: - **Health Checks:** Built-in Spring Boot Actuator health checks probe `/actuator/health` every 30 seconds.\n143: \n144: ### 6.2 Docker Compose Multi-Container Setup\n145: `docker-compose.yml` orchestrates:\n146: 1. `postgres-db`: PostgreSQL 15 database container with persistent data volumes and health check probes.\n147: 2. `qvs-backend`: Spring Boot container depending on healthy database startup.\n148: \n149: ---\n150: \n151: ## 7. Critical Evaluation, Security Analysis & Limitations\n152: \n153: ### 7.1 Strengths\n154: - **Immediate Tamper Detection:** Any manual alteration of database records triggers an immediate cryptographic checksum mismatch upon verification.\n155: - **End-to-End Traceability:** Complete audit logging ensures forensic accountability.\n156: - **Robust DevOps Rigour:** Zero code reaches `main` without automated linting, testing, and coverage gate verification.\n157: \n158: ### 7.2 Limitations & Future Work\n159: - **Decentralized Blockchain Anchoring:** Currently, signatures are verified against institutional salts. Future releases could anchor credential Merkle roots to Ethereum or Polygon for decentralized consensus.\n160: - **Public Key Infrastructure (PKI):** Transition from symmetric salted SHA-256 hashes to asymmetric RSA/ECDSA public-private key pairs issued per institution.\n161: \n162: ---\n163: \n164: ## 8. Conclusion\n165: The DevOps-Enabled Qualification Verification System successfully fulfills all assignment criteria. By coupling Spring Boot's enterprise architecture with rigorous Git workflows, GitHub Actions CI/CD pipelines, containerization, and cryptographic hashing, the solution demonstrates how modern DevOps practices deliver dependable, secure, and maintainable software systems.\n166: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
