{"step_index":183,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T17:19:40Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-06T19:19:40+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-06T19:19:40+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/Madziva/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/qualification-verification-system/docs/VIVA_DEMO_SCRIPT.md`\nTotal Lines: 76\nTotal Bytes: 4971\nShowing lines 1 to 60\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # VIVA & DEMONSTRATION SCRIPT (10–15 Minutes Presentation)\n2: \n3: **Project:** DevOps-Enabled Qualification Verification System (QVS)  \n4: **Target Duration:** 12–15 Minutes  \n5: **Audience:** Examiners, Academic Evaluators, and DevOps Assessors  \n6: \n7: ---\n8: \n9: ## ⏱ Time Allocation & Agenda Breakdown\n10: \n11: | Section | Duration | Key Focus Area | Presenter(s) |\n12: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n13: | **1. Introduction & Problem Context** | 2 mins | Credential fraud problem, project scope, architecture overview | Speaker 1 |\n14: | **2. Git Workflow & Collaboration** | 2.5 mins | GitFlow branching, PR reviews, merge conflict prevention, commit history | Speaker 2 |\n15: | **3. CI/CD Pipeline & Quality Gates** | 3.5 mins | GitHub Actions, Checkstyle, automated JUnit tests, JaCoCo coverage | Speaker 3 |\n16: | **4. Live System Demonstration** | 4.5 mins | Public verification, tamper detection, qualification issuance, audit trail | Speaker 4 / All |\n17: | **5. Reflection, Q&A, and Conclusions** | 2.5 mins | Lessons learnt, security considerations, limitations & future work | All Members |\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 🎬 Step-by-Step Viva Presentation Script\n22: \n23: ### Part 1: Introduction & Problem Context (0:00 - 2:00)\n24: - **Visuals:** Title Slide -> System Architecture Diagram.\n25: - **Speaking Notes:**\n26:   > *\"Good morning, esteemed assessors. Today, our team is presenting the DevOps-Enabled Qualification Verification System. Academic and professional credential fraud poses severe reputational and financial risks worldwide. Our goal was to engineer a cloud-native, secure, and verifiable platform backed by modern DevOps practices.*\n27:   > *Our architecture utilizes Java 17 and Spring Boot 3 with a clean 4-tier layered design, containerized with Docker, and secured via SHA-256 cryptographic fingerprinting and JWT role-based access control.\"*\n28: \n29: ---\n30: \n31: ### Part 2: Git-Based Collaboration & Branching Strategy (2:00 - 4:30)\n32: - **Visuals:** GitHub Repository insights, Network graph, PR list.\n33: - **Speaking Notes:**\n34:   > *\"To ensure collaborative rigor across our team of engineers, we instituted a strict GitFlow branching model. All work occurred in isolated `feature/*` branches linked to GitHub issues.*\n35:   > *Pull Requests required at least one peer code review, adherence to our PR checklist, and automated CI pipeline sign-off before merging into `develop` and `main`. This eliminated merge conflicts and preserved a clean, traceable commit history.\"*\n36: \n37: ---\n38: \n39: ### Part 3: DevOps CI/CD Pipeline & Quality Assurance (4:30 - 8:00)\n40: - **Visuals:** GitHub Actions Pipeline run, JaCoCo Coverage Report, Checkstyle terminal output.\n41: - **Speaking Notes:**\n42:   > *\"Our Continuous Integration pipeline in GitHub Actions runs four distinct jobs upon every push and PR:*\n43:   > 1. *First, Static Code Analysis enforces Google Java style rules via Checkstyle.*\n44:   > 2. *Second, Automated Testing runs our JUnit 5 unit tests and MockMvc integration tests. A JaCoCo quality gate enforces a strict 80% code coverage threshold, automatically failing the build if untested regressions occur.*\n45:   > 3. *Third, Packaging generates the executable Spring Boot artifact.*\n46:   > 4. *Finally, Dockerization executes a multi-stage Docker build, producing an unprivileged, minimal JRE runtime image for secure deployment.\"*\n47: \n48: ---\n49: \n50: ### Part 4: Live System Demonstration (8:00 - 12:30)\n51: - **Visuals:** Live Web Portal at `http://localhost:8080` & Swagger UI.\n52: - **Step 1: Public Verification of Authentic Credential**\n53:   - Enter certificate `QVS-2024-BSC-8891` (Sarah Jenkins).\n54:   - Click **Verify Authenticity**.\n55:   - Show the **GENUINE & VALID** green badge and the matching SHA-256 cryptographic signature.\n56: - **Step 2: Verification of Revoked Credential**\n57:   - Enter certificate `QVS-2022-DIP-1109` (Marcus Vance).\n58:   - Show the **REVOKED CREDENTIAL** red badge and display the disciplinary audit reason.\n59: - **Step 3: Tamper-Resistance Demonstration (Bonus Feature)**\n60:   - Explain how modifying any student record attribute causes an immediate cryptographic hash mismatch, flagging the record as **TAMPERED / SUSPICIOUS**.\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
